erlying assumptions and  calculations  well in a dpcr reaction the complete  reaction  is separated into multiple single  partitions  the target molecules are randomly  distributed across  all available partitions  now after this distribution process the  partitions will contain  either no copy of the target molecule  one copy of the target molecule or  more than one copy of the target  molecule  as the target molecules are distributed  randomly  poisson distribution can be used to  calculate the average copy of the target  molecule per valid partition  simeon dini person a french  mathematician  has developed a theory and corresponding  equations on the discrete probability  distribution  of a given number of events which can be  observed within a defined time  or as in our case space interval  our space interval will be a single  partition  now the poisson distribution itself  gives probabilities for positive integer  random events in this case a partition  containing at least one copy  of our target sequence lambda  is the expectation value for these  events and will tell  what the most likely average value of  copies per partition is  in order to do these calculations the  software needs the information how many  valid and how many positive partitions  we see in the well  the equation the equation shown also  demonstrates  why we always need at least a few  negative partitions in dpcr  in order to do proper calculations  if each valid partition were  a positive partition then the numerator  of this equation would become zero  and no further calculation would be  possible  i now would like to cover a few  applications which we had been calling  second level analyses all second level  analyses  are based on the results of the absolute  quantification  types of second level analyses include  mutation detection  gene expression analysis copy number  variation analysis and quantification of  genome editing events  for mutation detection copy numbers  of the wild type and the mutant target  which are detected on  different target channels are calculated  then the ratio of mutant versus total  number of positive events  including y type plus mutant is  calculated in  expressed in percent as fractional  abundance if replicates have been used  the mean ratio is also calculated in  expressed in percentage  let me explain it with sample number one  we have detected two thousand copies per  microliter of wild type  and 5000 copies of mutant  they sum up to 7 000 copies per  microliter  the fractional abundance of the mutant  is 71 percent  in sample two the copies of both targets  are much lower  still the fractional mutation abundance  is identical to sample number one  and we could go through all of those  samples but this would take quite some  time  therefore i rather proceed to the next  type of second level  analysis namely gene expression  for gene expression analysis the copies  per microliter of the gene of  interest in the reference gene or the  reference  genes are calculated the number of  reference genes can vary and usually  will be in the range of one to three  within one sample there can be multiple  genes of interest  or and reference genes each detected on  a different channel  then the ratio of gene of interest  versus reference gene  is calculated if more than one  reference gene has been used the ratio  of gene of interest  versus the geometric mean of the  reference genes  is calculated subsequently  the ratio of gene of interest versus  reference gene of the reference sample  is set to one the ratio of  all other samples are normalized to this  value and let me again explain it with  our sample one  we have detected two thousand copies per  migrator of our gene of interest  and found one thousand copies of our  reference g  number one from this we obtain  a ratio of two since sample  one is our reference sample which we  want to compare  all our other samples to we set this  ratio  to one if we now look at  our sample two the copies of the  reference gene  is identical to sample number one  however  our our gene of interest has  4 000 copies per microliter in this  sample  the ratio now will be 4  and if we compare it to our  reference sample  we will learn that our gene of interest  is  upregulated twofold  the way how we perform copy number  variation analysis is quite similar  copy numbers of the gene of interest and  the reference target are calculated then  the ratio of gene of interest versus  reference target is calculated  the ratio of gene of interest versus  reference target of the  reference sample is then set to the  known copy number  of this reference sample in this case  2 the ratios of all other samples are  compared to  compared to this reference sample and  the corresponding copy numbers  automatically will be calculated as it  is seen here  with this slide i would like to briefly  discuss  two edge cases when almost  all valid partitions are either positive  or negative  so so the basically all of them are  positive  basically all of them are negative then  the uncertainty of the calculation a  calculated concentration will clearly  increase  the confidence interval easily can  exceed 100 percent and certain  modifications  of the algorithms will apply  now the sweet spot for minimal  confidence interval values  lies in the range of one to two copies  on  average per partition but quantification  can range from  five copies per partition on average  down  even to one single copy in the whole  reaction  in the last part of my presentation i  would like to walk you through the  various analysis modules  and displays of the kai acuity software  the kai i would like to demonstrate the  various ways how  dpcr results can be displayed  in this webinar i will focus on absolute  quantification since the subsequent  analysis modules  will be explained in dedicated webinars  anyway  and don't forget absolute quantification  is the starting point for all  subsequent analyses  in order to analyze a plate you go to  the plates  overview page search for the plate you  want to perform an  analysis and select analyze from the  context menu  you will be encouraged to select the  corresponding wells  and you can select whether you would  like to perform a  channel or a target based analysis  this day in this case we decided to do  it based on the target we had been  looking for of course you can select  also multiple targets or channels  simultaneously and all the results will  be displayed in the  win in the results window  how will the results be displayed well  first of all there is the list view  it will display a table giving an  overview of the wells analyzed  this table will contain the following  information  the well identifier like a1 b2  the reaction mix it contains the icon  and the name of direction mix of course  it will do so only if you have defined  it previously  so there's no need to do so but  i can tell you it's quite helpful then  the next column will  contain the target or channel depending  on the  on the settings defined when selecting a  source this column shows then  the target or channel names  the sample ntc slash control designation  this column shows the sample entity and  control name with its corresponding icon  that identifies the sample or indicates  whether the entry is a negative control  no template control  or other type of control  the concentration given in copies per  microliter  this column shows the concentration  assigned to each target or channel per  well  if we had identified within one  and the same well uh various targets  because we performed a  multiplex experiment those ones would be  all listed in addition  the confidence interval uh  shows the value of the confidence  interval at a 95 percent confidence  level  if replicates have been used and  replicates are defined by using  the identical reaction mix plus the  identical samples  then you also could see the mean value  of those replicates last but not least  the partitions  this column shows the number of valid  positive and negative partitions per  well  and target or channel  on the heat map you will see the  individual quantification results for  each sample  given in copies per microliter  below that concentration  you will find the corresponding  confidence interval  given in percentages if you activate the  partitions u  the number of positive partitions will  be displayed  and please note that the number of  positive partitions does  not automatically uh  correspond to the copies per microliter  and below the number you will see the  number of  valid partitions in this case clearly  above  8 000 8 thousand of  8500 available ones  on the the histogram  view visualizes the fluorescence values  of selected wells  and the frequencies thereof for the  selected target or channel  it has two axis the x-axis  represents the relative fluorescence  intensity  the y-axis represents the number of  partitions showing that fluorescence  intensities  a threshold field shows the threshold  value of the fluorescence intensity  that is used to distinguish between  positive  and negative calls there are two ways to  change the threshold  the first one involves pointing on the  chart  which triggers the appearance of a  dotted line once the dotted line  is in the appropriate spot click the  chart  the line becomes solid as we see it here  and this threshold value is updated and  displayed in the threshold field  in addition to moving the line you also  can directly  edit the value in the threshold box  the 1d scatter plot tab shows  one 1d scatterplot view for each  analyzed target or channel  a 1d scatterplot view again has two axes  the x-axis represents the analyzed  partitions  while the y-axis represents the  relative fluorescence intensity of each  single partition  a red line represents the current  threshold fluorescence intensity value  that is used to distinguish between  positive  and negative partitions fluorescence  values  below the threshold are shown in gray  above the threshold in blue color  the threshold field shows the threshold  value of the fluorescence intensity that  is used to distinguish between positive  and negative call  by default the software will set the  threshold  automatically there are two ways to  change that preset for the threshold the  first one again  involves pointing to the chart which  triggers the appearance of a dotted line  once the dotted line is in the  appropriate spot click the chart  and the line b will become solid and the  threshold value is updated and displayed  in the threshold field  in addition to moving the line you also  can directly edit the value in the  threshold box  to apply the new value on the data click  recalculate to trigger the recalculation  of results  now this slide depicts the 1d scatter  plot display of an eva green based  experiment  where we had been using strongly varying  input of genomic dna  as you've already learned either green  based detection will also detect varying  gdna template  background and therefore it make perfect  sense  that the software set individual  thresholds  for the samples  now the concentration diagram shows the  diagram  that displays the consideration values  and confidence intervals for the samples  analyzed  one diagram is created again for each  selected target or channel  a concentration diagram has two  axis the x-axis shows the analyzed welds  and the y-axis represents the  concentration value  for the selected targets or channels of  each well  the values on the y-axis have two  available scales  either linear or logarithmic  each diagram represents two values for  each well  the concentration value which is the  displayed as a bar and the confidence  interval  displayed as an error bar to view the  mean values for replicates on the  diagram  click on show mean values for replicates  in this case  one bar is displayed for replicate group  showing the mean concentration value and  the mean ci value of the replicate group  the few exact values and the list of  valves belonging to the replicate group  you can simply point to the associated  bar  and the pop-up window will appear  the signal map view will display  all the partitions of a well which we  see here  which have been identified as positive  partitions  comparing them to the raw image you  occasionally will observe that  obvious dust bar particles as shown here  and other minute contaminations will be  detected automatically by the software  suite  regions where these artifacts can be  detected will be excluded  from analysis  the kaiakuiti's software suite allows  you to create reports of your  analysis results all created reports  remain  accessible in the software suite and can  be downloaded  or deleted at any time it  automatically includes run details such  as start and end time and software and  instrument version  you can include any diagram as long as  you don't exceed the number of 24.  the report is automatically downloaded  in the download  folder of the pc as a pdf file  furthermore the report is stored in  software suite and accessible via the  report environment  of the plate and with this slide  i would like to finish my presentation  and i'm looking forward  to taking your questions so thank you  very much for your attention
 

